[Step 550] Q: Let $p(x) = 2x - 7$ and $q(x) = 3x - b$.  If $p(q(4)) = 7$, what is $b$?
Ground truth: 5
Response[0]:
<start_working_out>

To find the value of $b$, we first need to evaluate the expression $p(q(4))$.

$q(4) = 3(4) - b = 12 - b$

Then, $p(q(4)) = p(12 - b) = 2(12 - b) - 7$

$p(q(4)) = 24 - 2b - 7 = 17 - 2b$

Since we know that $p(q(4)) = 7$, we can set up the equation:

$17 - 2b = 7$

Subtracting 17 from both sides of the equation, we get:

$-2b = -10$

Dividing both sides by -2, we get:

$b = 5$

